#: ../chapter03.rst:72
msgid "The team folder concept is like a network share, meaning you can define a folder and then add users and groups to the folder and thus turn it into a team shared folder. The team folder will show up in the user's folder list when the user is added to the team folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../chapter03.rst:77
msgid "ENTER TEAM FOLDER SECTION"
msgstr ""

#: ../chapter03.rst:79
msgid "When the server agent is in use, the team folder can be mapped directly to a network share from the server where the server agent is installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../chapter03.rst:81
msgid "When a directly connected network share is used, a team folder can be mapped to an SMB/CIFS network share directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../chapter03.rst:83
msgid "You can also turn any existing folder into a team folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../chapter03.rst:85
msgid "A Team folder has a tenant administrator scope so the team folder related sharing is limited to the users inside the tenant."
msgstr ""

#: ../chapter03.rst:89
msgid "By default, the files and folders that the administrator can see is hidden away from the regular team user until those folders are published to the team users."
msgstr ""

#: ../chapter03.rst:91
msgid "Team Folders (Shared Work Space) are used for team-share collaborations. Generally, Team Folders are converted from File Server Network shares. Other Team Folder sources can be Google Storage, Amazon S3 (or S3 Compatible), Amazon Cloud, Windows Azure Blob, WebDav, SharePoint, Rackspace (US or UK) and OpenStack or you can create new folders under the Tenant's root storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../chapter03.rst:93
msgid "In the \"Team Folders\" page, you can click \"Edit\" icon to manage team shares, folder permissions and the underlying storage configuration."
